# Net-classification-by-substring gate allowlist
# (scripts/check_net_classification.py). See
# docs/evidence/2026-07-27-net-classification-gate.md.
#
# Format: <qualname>::<repo-relative file glob>  # justification
# (required -- unjustified entries, or entries with no file-glob scope,
# are a hard error; see load_allowlist() in the gate script). Both the
# qualname and the file glob must match for an entry to apply -- a bare
# qualname is deliberately not supported, so allowlisting a known gap in
# one function cannot silently exempt an unrelated future function of the
# same name elsewhere. Wildcards (fnmatch-style) are supported in both
# fields, e.g. "Class.*" or "path/to/*.py".
#
# Every entry below was reviewed by hand and is a genuine non-safety use:
# the flagged code does not classify a *net name* into the HV/SELV
# electrical domain that elec/domain_manifest.yaml declares. Do not add an
# entry here to punt on a real net-classification bug -- fix it instead
# (word-boundary regex, or classify from elec/domain_manifest.yaml's exact
# net-name set, per the two historical fixes this gate is modeled on).

# Operates on a component REFERENCE DESIGNATOR (e.g. "R_GATE1"), not a net
# name -- classifying whether a resistor is a "gate resistor" by its own
# ref string. Component ref designators are not declared anywhere in
# elec/domain_manifest.yaml (which declares net names only), so there is
# no SSOT for this check to drift from in the way the historical defect
# class describes.
classify_component::packages/temper-placer/src/temper_placer/core/loop_extractor.py  # matches a component ref designator ("R_GATE1"), not a net name -- no domain_manifest.yaml counterpart

# Both functions parse a small, closed CONFIG-SCHEMA vocabulary (a string
# like "high_voltage"/"hv"/"selv" written by a human authoring a net-class
# YAML config), not a net's own NAME pulled from the compiled netlist.
# The subject string is the classification value itself, not something
# whose accidental substring content could misclassify an unrelated net --
# there is no net name involved on either side of the `in` test.
_parse_net_type::packages/temper-placer/src/temper_placer/core/net_types.py  # parses a config-schema keyword value ("high_voltage"/"hv"), not a net name
_parse_voltage_class::packages/temper-placer/src/temper_placer/core/net_types.py  # parses a config-schema keyword value ("selv"/"240"/"120"), not a net name

# Classifies a connector's PURPOSE (power_input / power_output / debug /
# signal) for PLACEMENT heuristics only (where to position a connector
# relative to the board edge). Does not feed any clearance/creepage/DRC
# safety decision -- elec/domain_manifest.yaml's HV/SELV boundary is not
# what this determines. A misclassification here is a placement-quality
# regression, not a safety gap.
_classify_connector_purpose::packages/temper-placer/src/temper_placer/heuristics/structural.py  # connector-purpose placement heuristic only; does not feed clearance/creepage/DRC

# Operates on an arbitrary, externally-supplied REFERENCE DESIGN used for
# quality-config auto-inference/benchmarking -- not this project's own
# board. Reference designs have no elec/domain_manifest.yaml counterpart
# to parallel (the manifest declares this project's own compiled nets
# only), so this cannot drift from an SSOT that does not exist for it.
infer_quality_config::packages/temper-placer/src/temper_placer/io/reference_loader.py  # operates on arbitrary external reference designs, not this project's board; no domain_manifest.yaml to parallel

# Diagnostic heuristic identifying which components to prioritize in
# routing-failure FEEDBACK (the placer's iterative repair loop), not a
# clearance/creepage safety verification path. A misclassification here
# affects which component gets highlighted in a diagnostic message, not
# whether a safety distance is enforced.
FeedbackClassifier._find_critical_components::packages/temper-placer/src/temper_placer/placer/cp_sat/feedback.py  # diagnostic routing-failure feedback heuristic, not a safety verification path

# Affects only the TIE-BREAK order nets are routed in within a conflict
# cluster (is_power as a tiebreaker after bottleneck-width/area) -- has no
# bearing on clearance/creepage safety verification, which is computed
# independently in clearance_check.py/creepage_check.py from
# elec/domain_manifest.yaml. A misclassification here changes routing
# sequence, never a reported safety distance.
_compute_net_order.cluster_sort_key::packages/temper-placer/src/temper_placer/router_v6/_astar_ordering.py  # routing-order tiebreaker only; independent of the safety verification path

# `loops` is a dict keyed by loop-name strings; `"dc_bus" in loops` is
# exact dict-KEY membership, not substring-of-string matching -- the same
# Compare(in) AST shape as the real defect class but semantically
# unrelated (dict `in` never does substring matching). Documented here
# rather than silently special-cased in the detector: distinguishing
# dict/set/list membership from string substring testing purely
# statically (without type inference) is a known limitation of this gate,
# not a claim that this code is otherwise verified safe.
check_critical_loop_areas::packages/temper-placer/tests/requirements/validators/layout.py  # dict-key membership test ("dc_bus" in a loop-name-keyed dict), not string substring matching

# Checks SILKSCREEN WARNING-LABEL TEXT content (e.g. "DANGER: HIGH
# VOLTAGE") for required-marking phrases -- verifying a physical safety
# marking is present on the board, not classifying a net's electrical
# domain. Substring/phrase-presence matching is the correct tool for
# free-form label text (unlike net-name classification, where the same
# technique causes accidental word-fragment collisions); "HV" as a
# substring of a whole warning sentence is the intended match, not an
# accident.
check_hv_warning_present::packages/temper-placer/tests/requirements/validators/markings.py  # matches silkscreen warning-label text content, not a net name

# Checks a component's BOM "value" field text (e.g. "UCC21550") for
# gate-driver-IC keyword presence, to confirm a gate driver IC exists in
# the schematic -- not classifying a net name into an electrical domain.
check_gate_driver_enable::packages/temper-placer/tests/requirements/validators/schematic.py  # matches component BOM "value" text for IC-presence detection, not a net name
